openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL153: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? 【41†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org】.\nL154: \nL155: 【42†Which authors of this paper are endorsers?】 | Disable MathJax (【43†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL156: \nL157: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, 【44†member institutions†info.arxiv.org】, , and all contributors.\nL158: 【45†About†info.arxiv.org】 · 【46†Help†info.arxiv.org】 · 【47†Contact†info.arxiv.org】 · 【48†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org】 · 【49†Copyright†info.arxiv.org】 · 【50†Privacy†info.arxiv.org】 · 【51†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org】 · 【52†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org】\nL159: \nL160: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOpen Problems on Configuration Spaces of Tensegrities | Arnold Mathematical Journal | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40598-018-0080-7)\nciteturn1339view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40598-018-0080-7\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 337\nL0: Account\nL1: \nL2: 【0†Menu】\nL3: \nL4: 【1†Find a journal】 【2†Publish with us†www.springernature.com】 【3†Track your research†link.springernature.com】\nL5: \nL6: 【4†Search】\nL7: \nL8: 【5†Saved research】\nL9: \nL10: 【6†Cart†order.springer.com】\nL11: # Open Problems on Configuration Spaces of Tensegrities\nL12: \nL13:   * Problem Contribution\nL14:   * 【7†Open access†www.springernature.com】\nL15:   * Published: 02 February 2018\nL16: \nL17:   * Volume 4, pages 19–25 (2018)\nL18:   * 【8†Cite this article】\nL19: \nL20: You have full access to this open access article\nL21: \nL22: 【9†Download PDF】\nL23: \nL24: [Input] Save article\nL25: \nL26: 【5†View saved research】\nL27: \nL28: 【10†Arnold Mathematical Journal】 【11†Aims and scope】\nL29: \nL30: Open Problems on Configuration Spaces of Tensegrities\nL31: \nL32: 【9†Download PDF】\nL33: \nL34:   * 【12†Oleg Karpenkov】^{【13†1】}\nL35:   * 4250 Accesses\nL36: \nL37:   * 1 Altmetric\nL38: \nL39:   * 【14†Explore all metrics】\nL40: ## Abstract\nL41: In this small paper we bring together some open problems related to the study of the configuration spaces of tensegrities, i.e. graphs with stresses on edges. These problems were announced in Doray et al. (Discrete Comput Geom 43:436–466, 【15†2010】), Karpenkov et al. (ARS Math Contemp 6:305–322, 【16†2013】), Karpenkov (The combinatorial geometry of stresses in frameworks.\nL42: 【17†arXiv:1512.02563†arxiv.org】 [math.MG], 【18†2017】), and Karpenkov (Geometric Conditions of Rigidity in Nongeneric settings, 【19†2016】) (by F. Doray, J. Schepers, B. Servatius, and the author), for more details we refer to the mentioned articles.\nL43: ### Similar content being viewed by others\nL44: \nL45: 【20†Image†media.springernature.com】\nL46: \nL47: ### 【21†Tensegrity topology optimization by force maximization on arbitrary ground structures】\nL48: \nL49: Article 31 January 2019\nL50: \nL51: 【22†Image†media.springernature.com】\nL52: \nL53: ### 【23†On Directed Densest Subgraph Detection】\nL54: \nL55: Chapter © 2024\nL56: \nL57: 【24†Image†media.springernature.com】\nL58: \nL59: ### 【25†Local Properties in Colored Graphs, Distinct Distances, and Difference Sets】\nL60: \nL61: Article 11 February 2019\nL62: \nL63: ## 1 Notation and Definitions\nL64: ### 1.1 Tensegrities\nL65: \nL66: The subject of tensegrities was first considered by J.C. Maxwell in Maxwell (【26†1864】), who started to investigate first questions regarding force-loads for frameworks. Nowadays tensegrities are one of the leading directions of study in modern theory of rigidity (see, e.g., Connelly (【27†1993】) for further information). Let us recall several standard definitions.\nL67: ### Definition 1.1\nL68: \nL69: Fix a positive integer d. Let \\(G=(V,E)\\) be an arbitrary graph without loops and multiple edges. Let it have n vertices \\(v_1,\\ldots , v_n\\).\nL70: \nL71:   * A configuration is a finite collection P of n labeled points \\((p_1,p_2,\\ldots ,p_n)\\), where each point \\(p_i\\) is in a fixed Euclidean space \\(\\mathbb {R}^d\\).\nL72:   * The realization of G with straight edges, induced by mapping \\(v_j\\) to \\(p_j\\) is called a tensegrity framework and it is denoted as G(P). (Here we allow the realization to have self-intersections).\nL73: \nL74:   * A stress w on a framework is an assignment of real scalars \\(w_{i,j}\\) (called tensions) to its edges \\(p_ip_j\\).\nL75: \nL76:   * A stress w is called a self-stress if at every vertex \\(p_i\\) we have\nL77: \nL78: $$\\begin{aligned} \\sum \\limits _{\\{j|j\\ne i\\}} w_{i,j}(p_j-p_i)=0. \\end{aligned}$$\nL79:   * A pair (G(P), w) is a tensegrity if w is a self-stress for the framework G(P).\nL80: \nL81:   * If \\(w_{i,j} {<} 0\\) then we call the edge \\(p_ip_j\\) a cable, if \\(w_{i,j} {>} 0\\) we call it a strut.\nL82: ### 1.2 Configuration Space of Tensegrities and its Stratification\nL83: \nL84: Denote by \\(B_d(G)=(\\mathbb {R}^d)^n\\) the configuration space of all tensegrity frameworks. Let W(n) denote the linear space with coordinates \\(w_{i,j}\\) where \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\). It is clear that \\(\\dim W(n)=n^2\\).\nL85: \nL86: ### Definition 1.2\nL87: \nL88: Consider a framework \\(G(P)\\in B_d(G)\\) and denote by W(G, P) the linear subspace of W(n) of all possible self-stresses for G(P). The space W(G, P) is the fiber at P.\nL89: ### Definition 1.3\nL90: \nL91: Fibers \\(W(G,P_1)\\) and \\(W(G,P_2)\\) are said to be equivalent if there exists a homeomorphism \\(\\xi :W(G,P_1)\\rightarrow W(G,P_2)\\), such that for any \\(w\\in W(G,P_1)\\) we have\nL92: \nL93: $$\\begin{aligned} {{\\mathrm{sgn}}}\\big (\\xi (w_{i,j})\\big )={{\\mathrm{sgn}}}\\big (w_{i,j}\\big ) \\end{aligned}$$\nL94: \nL95: for every coordinate \\(w_{i,j}\\) of w. Here \\({{\\mathrm{sgn}}}\\) denotes the standard sign function.\nL96: The described equivalence relation gives us a stratification of \\(B_d(G)=(\\mathbb {R}^d)^n.\\) A stratum is by definition a maximal connected component of \\(B_d(G)\\) with equivalent fibers. In Doray et al. (【15†2010】) we prove that all strata are semialgebraic sets.\nL97: ### Remark\nL98: \nL99: According to all known examples the majority of the strata of codimension k are intersections of the strata of codimension 1 see e.g. Doray et al. (【15†2010】), Karpenkov et al. (【16†2013】). So the most important case to study is the codimension 1 case.\nL100: ### Remark\nL101: \nL102: A stratification of a subgraph is a substratification of the original graph (i.e., each stratum for a subgraph is the union of certain strata for the original graph). Hence the case of complete graphs \\(K_n\\) is universal. This is straightforward as each extra edge contributes at least as much to dimensions of the fibers of a stratum as to the dimensions of the fibers of adjacent strata (locally).\nL103: ### Example 1.4\nL104: \nL105: Let us consider a simple example of \\(B_1(K_{3})\\), namely we study tensegrities for a complete graph on three vertices and its realizations in the line. We assume that the line has a coordinate, so each point of \\(B_1(K_3)\\) is associated with three coordinates \\((x_1,x_2,x_3)\\).\nL106: \nL107: First we study a particular case \\(x_1=0\\), which we denote by \\(B_1^0(K_3)\\). The set \\(B_1^0(K_3)\\) has the following stratification (see Fig. 【28†1】, Left):\nL108:   * 1 stratum of codimension two: the origin. Here all three vertices coincide and the dimension of the fiber is 3.\nL109: \nL110:   * 6 strata of codimension one: some pair of vertices coincide. The dimension of fiber is two.\nL111: \nL112:   * 6 connected components of full dimension correspond to triple of distinct vertices. The dimension of a fiber is one.\nL113: It is clear that the dimension of the fiber for \\((x_1,x_2,x_3)\\) coincides with the dimension of the fiber \\((0,x_2-x_1,x_3-x_1)\\) for every \\(x_1,x_2,x_3\\). Therefore, we have\nL114: \nL115: $$\\begin{aligned} B_1(K_3)=B^0_1(K_3)\\times \\mathbb {R}^1. \\end{aligned}$$\nL116: \nL117: Fig. 1\nL118: \nL119: 【29†Image: Fig. 1†media.springernature.com】\nL120: \nL121: 【30†Full size image】\nL122: \nL123: Stratification of \\(B^0_1(K_3)\\) (left) and \\(B^0_1(G_{1,2-3})\\) (right)\nL124: ### Example 1.5\nL125: \nL126: Let now \\(G_{1,2-3}\\) be the graph on three vertices \\(v_1,v_2,v_3\\) with the only edge connecting \\(v_2\\) and \\(v_3\\). Then we have 1 stratum of codimension one defined by \\(p_2=p_3\\) (with fibers of dimension 1) and two connected components in the complement, i.e., where \\(p_2\\ne p_3\\) (with fibers of dimension 0). As in previous example we have\nL127: \nL128: $$\\begin{aligned} B_1(G_{1,2-3})=B^0_1(G_{1,2-3})\\times \\mathbb {R}^1, \\end{aligned}$$\nL129: where \\(B^0_1(G_{1,2-3})\\) is the stratification of the section \\(x_1=0\\) (see Fig. 【28†1】, Right).\nL130: ## 2 Combinatoric Properties of Stratification\nL131: In Example 【31†1.4】 above we discussed a stratification of \\(B_2(K_n)\\). The most important information on the stratification contains its combinatorial structure, namely the list of strata of different dimensions and the adjacency diagram for the strata. For the combinatorial description of \\(B_2(K_n)\\) as in Example 【31†1.4】, for \\(n=2,3,4,5\\) we refer to Karpenkov et al. (【16†2013】). There is not much known about more complicated configuration spaces.\nL132: The next simplest and most interesting unstudied cases are listed in the following problem.\nL133: ### Problem 1\nL134: \nL135: Describe the combinatorics of \\(B_2(K_6), B_3(K_4)\\) and \\(B_3(K_5)\\).\nL136: \nL137: As experiments show, for every codimension 1 stratum there exists a certain subgraph that locally identifies the stratum (i.e., for every point x of the stratum there exists a neighborhood B(x) such that every configuration in B(x) has a nonzero self-stress for the subgraph if and only if this point is on the stratum). This observation is valid for all dimensions d.\nL138: ### Remark 2.1\nL139: \nL140: An illustration here are the cases \\(B_1(K_3)\\) and \\(B_1(G_{1,2-3})\\) of Examples 【31†1.4】 and 【32†1.5】. Here both codimension 1 strata of \\(B_1(K_3)\\) corresponding to \\(p_2=p_3\\) are in the union of the codimension 1 stratum of \\(B_1(G_{1,2-3})\\). This is a general situation. In particular for any graph G on n vertices the union of all strata for G is contained in the union of all strata for \\(K_n\\).\nL141: Let us say a few words about two-dimensional tensegrities. In the paper Doray et al. (【15†2010】) one can find the classification of all strata of codimension 1 for \\(n\\le 8\\) points. For further examples, see the papers of White and Whiteley (【33†1983】), and de Guzmán and Orden (【34†2004】), Guzmán and Orden (【35†2006】). In the paper Karpenkov (【18†2017】) it was shown how to approach every stratum for the case \\(n=9\\). The next case which contains unknown strata is \\(n=10\\) (see also Problem 【36†8】 below).\nL142: ### Problem 2\nL143: \nL144: Describe all the possible different types of strata for 10 points.\nL145: \nL146: ### Problem 3\nL147: \nL148: Compute the number of different types of strata for n points with arbitrary n.\nL149: \nL150: In many cases the strata for different graphs coincide. So it is natural to ask the following question.\nL151: \nL152: ### Problem 4\nL153: \nL154: Which subgraphs of \\(K_n\\) define the same stratifications?\nL155: \nL156: Finally the following question remains open.\nL157: ### Problem 5\nL158: \nL159: Find all strata of codimension more than 1 that are not defined as an intersection of the closure of several codimension 1 strata.\nL160: \nL161: Here we have only a trivial example of a graph on 2 vertices and one edge in the plane. It has one generic stratum of full dimension and one stratum of codimension 2, corresponding to two coinciding points.\nL162: ## 3 Geometric Conditions Defining Strata in \\(\\mathbb {R}^2\\)\nL163: \nL164: Majority of known examples in the planar case are expressed in terms of Cayley algebra. Recall that the objects of (planar) Cayley algebra are points and lines in the plane. Cayley algebra has two major operations:\nL165: \nL166:   * join operation \\(\\vee \\) is defined for a pair of points. The resulting object is the line joining these points.\nL167:   * meet operation \\(\\wedge \\) is defined for a pair of lines. The meet of two lines is the intersection point of two lines.\nL168: \nL169: For more information on Cayley algebras we refer to Doubilet et al. (【37†1974】), White and McMillan (【38†1988】), Li (【39†2008】), and Karpenkov (【18†2017】).\nL170: \n"
